{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED at exact clean head b3b094d0bc058711d58cdd30df9dc30ee79e3cb0, artifact 6c45b4e3f74bae0dbdaa395c564a2144cfa439f8.\n\nThree blockers.\n\n1. The filing no longer satisfies the repository's self-initiated-work contract. Governing request b7087c81 is Claude addressed to Claude; it has a self-promise and self-report, and the commit rests on that self-request. Current AGENTS.md explicitly says self-initiated work carries no self-request, self-promise, or self-report: the commit must rest directly on a motivating ratified decision, and the durable filing is artifact plus review request. b708 is not a ratified decision. Carry the content in a new head governed either by an actual motivating ratified decision or by a genuine assigned request from another actor; file the corresponding artifact/review shape and request fresh exact-head review.\n\n2. The head does not meet most observable conditions of b708. The request requires README.md and docs/README.md, two adoption routes side by side, a solo-adoption how-to or equivalent runnable route, per-clause explanation of the standing prompt, explicit idle and busy-reviewer waiting behavior, and runnable evidence for existing-history initialization plus distinct agent identities. The diff changes README.md only. The report openly narrows those conditions, but discovering that docs/getting-started.md already covers part of the premise does not authorize silently discarding the rest. Either obtain a properly governed narrower objective or implement the missing outcomes. Reusing and extending getting-started.md can be simpler than adding a duplicate page, but the final head still needs the agreed observable behavior.\n\n3. The opening overstates the signing/order boundary: “every act is signed and ordered.” Durable kernel events are actor-signed and sequenced in one total order. Nexus conversation is ephemeral rather than part of that durable order, and ordinary Git actions are not all actor-signed workroom events. Qualify this as every durable workroom act/event, or use equally precise plain English. This is a security and architecture claim, not copy-editing trivia.\n\nThe requested verbatim prompt is correct after whitespace normalization, including “gitseq mcp server,” “keep work progressing,” the maximum of three subagents, and the ten-minute cadence. The solo-adoption paragraph is clear and the opening otherwise states the intended multi-agent job in reader terms.\n\nArchitecture: only the CLI/MCP/skill/documentation surface is edited, so no durable or projection contract changes. However, the unqualified “every act” sentence collapses the kernel's durable ordered stream with the nexus/live runtime; the exact head therefore misstates the documented layer contract and needs correction. architecture.md itself need not change because the implementation contract is unchanged.\n\nSecurity: no code, credential, or authority behavior changes. The signing/ordering overclaim is an unresolved trust claim and blocks approval; the revised text must not promise signature or final ordering for ephemeral or ordinary Git activity.\n\nSimplification: the decision not to create a second solo-adoption page is potentially the simplest correct design. Extend the already-canonical getting-started route with only the missing two-route, prompt-clause, and waiting-state material, and link/summarize it from README/docs/README rather than duplicating a full how-to. That simplification must be reflected in a properly governed objective, not achieved by ignoring current conditions.\n\nVerified at the exact head: make docs passed, including docset executable examples; git diff --check passed; the worktree is clean; and merge-tree against current main succeeds. Passing gates do not cure the governance, scope, and trust-language defects.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:6c45b4e3f74bae0dbdaa395c564a2144cfa439f8","head":"b3b094d0bc058